-- allium: 3
-- Opt-in approximate nearest-neighbour search over a table's vector column.
-- Table events and capture follow db.allium and allium/live-index.allium.
-- Generations are artefacts of kind `vector`: trie-cat.allium records them on trie entries, db.allium carries ArtefactsAdded through the leader, and gc.allium deletes them with their owner.
-- See rust/crates/vector, rust/crates/maintenance/src/artefact.rs and rust/crates/query/src/accelerate.rs.

entity VectorIndexDefinition {
    name: String
    relation: String
    column: String
    partition_columns: List<String>
    dimensions: Integer
    method: String
    recall: Decimal
    generation: Integer
}

entity VectorGeneration {
    definition: VectorIndexDefinition
    trie: String
    directory_digest: Bytes
}

entity ProbePlan {
    probe_fraction: Decimal
    shortlist: Integer
}

-- Definitions
--
-- `CREATE VECTOR INDEX name ON relation (column) PARTITION BY (columns) WITH (dimensions = n, method = 'ivf', recall = r)` declares a definition in `xt.vector_indexes`.
-- The generation is the declaring transaction, so a declaration after a drop has a fresh one and its artefact address.
-- `IF NOT EXISTS` leaves an existing definition and its generation in place.
-- Dropping a definition withdraws it at the ordered system cut; its generations become retired artefacts.
-- IVF is the only method.
-- An owned Root relation declares its approximate vector path with `rtdb.owned.DeclareVectorPath.v1` in the principal's `.vector-paths` catalog, with the same fields; its partition is the relation.
-- The method name and the generation file layout participate in every artefact address.
-- The Default shared-main projection supplies native IVF declarations to the existing generation writer.
-- The Multi writer publishes the exact merged-main primary trie inventory and its native IVF files together under the fenced storage generation.
-- Restore rebuilds that inventory at the destination basis under the destination writer and key generation.
-- Native readiness at the installed exact cut reopens every current-trie file for the declared generation and validates its directory and clusters.
-- The permitted exact SQL route reports approximate false.
-- COSINE_DISTANCE_F32 evaluates equal-dimension float32 vectors in component order with binary64 products and sums.
-- A zero vector has no distance, and a zero probe is invalid.
-- A bounded exact Query can select the effective filtered corpus before ranking, including replacements and tombstones staged by the caller.
-- Its total counts non-null distances before the requested window, and ties use the complete typed entity sort key.
-- Serving lookup validates the catalog rows and retains the selected definition under its own admission.
-- The artefact builder retains admitted definition metadata while enumerating jobs or building a generation.
--
-- Generations
--
-- A generation covers one live current trie and is a deterministic function of that trie's puts and the definition.
-- A put contributes its IID, valid interval, system-from, partition digest and unit vector.
-- Supported plain Arrow partition cells contribute the same digest as their SQL execution values, including signed-width interpretation.
-- Training normalizes supported Arrow numeric spans in execution order and interprets unsigned components at their signed execution width.
-- Deletes, erases and puts whose vector has no defined cosine distance contribute nothing.
-- Rows sort by partition, IID and temporal coordinates before training, so their input order does not reach the bytes.
-- Each partition trains its own clusters with spherical k-means: evenly spaced seeds, a fixed number of rounds, f64 sums in row order and ties to the lower cluster.
-- A partition of at most 256 rows is one cluster.
-- Each cluster starts at a 64-byte boundary and holds one Arrow IPC stream with 64-byte message and buffer alignment relative to that start.
-- The directory names every cluster's partition, centroid, byte range, row count and SHA-256 digest covering the stream through its end marker.
-- Generation staging admits row descriptors and normalized components before growing their buffers.
-- Each cluster streams its side columns and selected components in row order from the staged rows and arena.
-- A single-cluster partition writes its ordered member range directly.
-- Training and IPC scratch are scoped to the partition and stream that use them.
-- The directory admits retained rows and centroids as they accumulate.
-- The artefact record carries the directory's location and digest, and a reader checks every directory and cluster byte it uses against them.
-- The artefact lane builds generations for the tries `wants` admits and publishes them with ArtefactsAdded.
-- Compaction output gets its own generation; an erased version is absent from compacted tries and so from their generations.
-- A generation whose trie becomes garbage, or whose definition is retired, is deleted through the trie deletion grant like every other artefact.
--
-- The approximate read
--
-- A scan opts in with the predicate APPROXIMATE_NEAREST(column, probe, limit), which on a row holds where the column is not null.
-- Without a definition for the column, the scan evaluates the predicate row by row and ranks exactly.
-- The route applies only at a valid-time point and a system-time point, without transaction overlays.
-- The published frontier is the set of live current tries that carry a generation of the definition.
-- Tries above the frontier and the live segments are read exactly, up to a byte budget; beyond it the scan ranks exactly.
-- Source planning admits descriptor growth and can decline as soon as uncovered trie sizes exceed the budget.
-- An equality filter that fixes every partition column selects the clusters of that partition before any ranking.
-- An IID scope from another accelerator small enough to rank exactly leaves the scan exact.
-- Each generation probes its eligible clusters nearest centroid first; the recall target sets the starting probe fraction and shortlist.
-- Cluster reads limit concurrent range and decode work using available admission.
-- A read refused by concurrent memory pressure is retried alone after the other reads settle.
-- The directory is validated in place, including ascending partition order.
-- A partition filter seeks its contiguous directory row range.
-- The read reserves ranking scratch before retaining the eligible cluster descriptions.
-- The shortlist retains admitted normalized probe components and scores eligible borrowed frontier cells in component order.
-- Fallback vector and partition cells use individually admitted decode scratch.
-- The shortlist keeps the nearest distinct IIDs by unit-vector distance across generations and the frontier.
-- One funded score per distinct IID stays available for subsequent rounds, including IIDs omitted from a narrower round.
-- The scan uses exact ranking if another distinct IID would exceed the funded score bound.
-- A round hands its funded Arrow IID membership to the exact verifier.
-- The verifier resolves the shortlist at the read cut and applies every predicate of the scan.
-- Fewer visible candidates than the limit double the probe fraction and the shortlist until the limit is met, every cluster and row has been considered, or the shortlist would exceed its budget and the scan ranks exactly.
-- The scan reads only the shortlisted IIDs, resolves their visible versions and computes the exact COSINE_DISTANCE on their stored vectors.
-- An erased, deleted or superseded version never reaches a result, because resolution at the read cut decides visibility.
-- A missing or corrupt generation leaves the scan exact.
-- Results of the approximate route may omit true neighbours; their distances and order are exact.
--
-- The native v1 route
--
-- An explicit ranked IVF Query selects one declared generation at a tracked clear exact cut.
-- The reader retains the current primary catalogue paired with the native cut and opens every current-trie generation with the recorded provider identity.
-- The Multi reader verifies the encrypted publication and retains the authenticated bytes through candidate resolution and delivery.
-- The shortlist probes slices of the verified bytes held by that reader.
-- The reader requires complete persisted inventory and a covered temporal point.
-- Each round resolves the candidate IIDs at that cut, masks stored rows by complete typed identity, and filters the surviving rows and staged replacements before applying k.
-- The final order uses cosine distance on the original float32 components and the complete typed identity.
-- A replacement with absent or zero-norm embedding still masks its stored row.
-- The reply counts scored candidates and reports approximation when the settled shortlist leaves candidates unconsidered.
-- An unavailable generation proof or exhausted budget refuses ranked IVF execution.
--
-- The held route
--
-- `rtdb.owned.NearestHeld.v4` with the approximate route ranks, for each partition with an approximate vector path, the rows of its shortlist resolved at the held cut.
-- Each round projects only the shortlisted identities, applies the request's filters and computes exact distances; fewer than k passing rows widen the shortlist.
-- A partition without a path, or whose shortlist exceeds its budget, ranks exactly; the reply states whether any partition ranked a shortlist.
-- A memory refusal while merging a settled shortlist leaves the ranked window and candidate count unchanged for exact ranking.
